<article class="wrap legal-page" id="terms">
    <p class="kicker">Before you book</p>
    <h1>The terms of a Portico stay</h1>
    <p class="legal-intro">Portico keeps a short list of stays. These terms say what a price means, and what a reservation on this site does today.</p>
    <div class="legal-doc">
        <h2>The list</h2>
        <p>If a stay is not on the list, Portico is not offering it. The list, the photographs, and the prices can change. A house that cannot sleep your group stays on the list and says so.</p>

        <h2>The price</h2>
        <p>The stay total is the nightly rate times the number of nights times the number of rooms. The card states that this total includes taxes and charges. A price shown before you add dates is a nightly rate, not a total for a stay.</p>

        <h2>Cancellation</h2>
        <p>Where a room offers free cancellation, it runs until three days before check-in. A room that does not offer it is marked non-refundable. Some stays ask for a minimum number of nights. The property page states the rule that applies to that stay.</p>

        <h2>A reservation on this site</h2>
        <p>Confirm reservation does not hold a room and does not take payment. The reference it shows is a preview. A stay is booked only when Portico confirms it and a payment step exists.</p>

        <h2>The house</h2>
        <p>The house rules on the property page are part of that stay: check-in, check-out, children, pets, and quiet hours. You agree to those rules when a booking is later confirmed.</p>

        <h2>The account</h2>
        <p>Sign in and register on this site store nothing. They show the door an account will use later. See the <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Privacy">privacy notice</a> for what is kept in this browser, and what a later version will store.</p>

        <p class="legal-updated">Last updated 12 October 2026.</p>
    </div>
</article>
